dh07+@andrew.cmu.edu (David Hairston) writes:
>nh3 is clearly more featured {harder?} than previous hacks. however, one
>feature is overly-prominent: the ubiquitous doors. to my taste doors should
>be constrained to non-run-of-the-mill rooms and keys should appear more
>frequently. under this scheme the rooms containing the up or down stairwells,
>throne rooms, shops etc., could have doors and the rest would be as before
>with just open doorways. with a profusion of keys and lock types the hacker
>would have to choose what set of keys to carry and which to leave behind.
>in addition to humans, only intelligent monsters {wizard, shaman, etc.}
>that are carrying the right keys could open locked doors. shops would have
>a chance of being randomly closed for intervals during which any entry
>would be considered trespassing. in a related way, when the shopkeeper says
>the shop is closing in 50 {100 whatever} moves you'd better be outside by then
>or face the consequences. bumping into a door should have a chance of
>opening/breaking it as well as doing Hp damage.


Closets can be added: you find a locked door, and when you finally open it,
it turns out to be a closet. Some closets have things stored in them. A
very few closets really turn out to be express elevators.


Once in awhile you find a shop with a locked front door, a closed shop which
you can loot. An alarm may go off. If the shopkeeper says he's closing, and
you don't get out, keystone kops appear to escort you out.

Doors clearly have alot of potential!

